[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 475,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 432,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67428,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 432,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adult cellular rhabdomyoma is a benign neoplasm with striated muscle differentiation that occurs in the heart. It is the cardiac counterpart of extracardiac rhabdomyoma, adult type, which usually occurs in the head and neck region of adults aged &gt;&nbsp;40&nbsp;years.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 476,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 433,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67429,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 433,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8904/0 Adult cellular rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 477,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 434,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67430,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 434,
        "headingText": "<p>2F01 &amp; XH4BG5 Benign tumour of intrathoracic organs &amp; Adult rhabdomyoma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-01-08T10:33:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 478,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 435,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67431,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 435,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> adult intracardiac rhabdomyoma resembling the extracardiac tumour; adult rhabdomyoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 479,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 436,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67432,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 436,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67433,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular rhabdomyomas have been reported in the right atrium, tricuspid valve, atrial septum, right ventricle, and left ventricle {17727983; 23329642; 12454813; 25127964; 7397640; 8491485}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67434,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Clinical symptoms vary depending on intracardiac location. Patients with tumours in the atria present with supraventricular tachycardia and palpitations. Right ventricular tumours may be incidental findings or result in right bundle branch block. A left ventricular tumour caused dyspnoea on exertion and palpitations {25127964}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67435,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adult extracardiac rhabdomyoma is a rare soft tissue tumour, accounting for &lt;&nbsp;2% of soft tissue neoplasms. There are &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;reports of adult cellular rhabdomyoma involving the heart. The reported patient age range is 23&ndash;62&nbsp;years. There is no sex predilection among the few reported cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67436,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. There is no association with tuberous sclerosis or any syndrome. Adult cellular rhabdomyoma is not related to cardiac rhabdomyoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67437,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Reciprocal translocation of chromosomes&nbsp;15 and 17 and abnormalities in the long arm of chromosome&nbsp;10 have been described in a parapharyngeal rhabdomyoma {1530111}, but the pathogenesis of cardiac adult cellular rhabdomyoma is unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67438,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular rhabdomyomas are homogeneous, circumscribed fleshy tan masses that may be pedunculated and protrude into the atrium or ventricle or that are situated within the atrial septum. They are usually smooth, but a lobulated surface has also been described {25127964}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67439,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adult cellular rhabdomyomas are similar in appearance to adult extracardiac rhabdomyoma {12454813; 8491485}. The tumours are composed of small round, ovoid, or spindled cells that form sheets, with prominent vascularity. Sarcoplasmic vacuolization is usually prominent. Cross-striations may be seen. Tumour cells express skeletal muscle markers such as myogenin immunohistochemically, in addition to desmin. Occasionally, perinuclear haloes and multinucleation occur, resulting in cells resembling the spider cells seen in cardiac rhabdomyoma. However, classic spider cells are absent. There is a low but significant proliferation index by immunostaining for Ki-67, unlike in cardiac rhabdomyoma, which is non-reactive {12454813}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67440,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytological diagnosis of adult cellular rhabdomyoma has not been described. Aspirates of extracardiac rhabdomyoma of the adult type show clusters and isolated polygonal cells with abundant granular cytoplasm and small peripherally located nuclei {32187885; 29131558}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 487,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 444,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67441,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 444,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The diagnostic molecular pathology has not been described. Unlike in cardiac rhabdomyoma, there are no alterations in <em>TSC1</em> or <em>TSC2</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 490,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 447,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67442,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 447,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Cellular neoplasm expressing skeletal muscle markers</li>\r\n<li>Age &gt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;years</li>\r\n<li>Absence of signs of tuberous sclerosis</li>\r\n<li>Absence of pleomorphism, mitotic activity, or necrosis</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Ki-67 proliferation index &gt;&nbsp;1% and &lt;&nbsp;20%</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 488,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 445,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67443,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 445,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 489,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 446,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67444,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 136,
        "headingId": 446,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No patient has been described with recurrence to date. Extracardiac rhabdomyomas of the adult type recur uncommonly.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:39:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]